1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,310
The grave of a pilot who lay in his plane for 73 years.

2
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:11,920
In March 2017, 14-year-old Daniel Rom Kristiansen and his father

3
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:18,470
were testing a metal detector on the family field near Birkelse in North Jutland, for a school project.

4
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:21,010
The detector kept going off.

5
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:25,940
They dug with an excavator and, seven to eight metres down,

6
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:29,240
hit the wreckage of a Messerschmitt Bf 109,

7
00:00:29,260 --> 00:00:35,530
the Luftwaffe's most important fighter, of which more than 33,000 were built.

8
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:37,810
But there was more in the wreck:

9
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:40,750
the remains of the pilot.

10
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:47,850
Next to his bones, archaeologists from the North Jutland Historical Museum found personal belongings,

11
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:51,950
a wallet, identity papers and remnants of his uniform.

12
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:57,850
With their help and German military records, the pilot was identified:

13
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:03,310
Hans Wunderlich, born on 22 July 1925.

14
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:11,190
He was 19 when his plane crashed near Aalborg on 10 October 1944.

15
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:16,990
Whether air combat or a technical fault caused it remains unclear to this day.

16
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:23,680
The damp, oxygen-poor soil had preserved the wreck and the remains for more than seven decades.

17
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:29,490
73 years after his death, Hans Wunderlich was finally laid to rest.
